WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the rural community of paine is home to  a brutal statistic  the largest number of victims per capita  of chile's former military dictatorship  65 year old sonia valenzuela still lives  in what's called widow's lane she was 16  when soldiers raided the street and took  her father her four uncles and her  husband of only six months  from the winter i saw that they were  taking away a line of people with their  hands up like this  my uncle cried a lot he said sonja  they're going to kill me and so it was  yes  the majority of those who lived here in  1973 were poor illiterate farmers who'd  benefited from a land reform program  [Music]  until a military coup turned the clock  back  in all 70 men were taken from here and  disappeared leaving their families  penniless  we were destitute my grandmother was  left to care for 16 grandchildren they  asked for milk for bread  but the worst part she says was the  search for their bodies and for justice  justice was finally served this week to  38 of the 70 victims who are honored at  this memorial  after numerous appeals the supreme court  has ruled to imprison 11 soldiers and  policemen with sentences of up to 20  years  lost two brothers two uncles and her  father  she says she's waited nearly 50 years to  be able to sleep in peace  i think of my beloved mother who died in  december i can say that i completed my  mission for her she fought for so many  years for this  yet only 33 of the victims remains have  been found  24 of them were executed and thrown into  a ravine  lengthened  they only found my father's glasses and  a bit of clothing the rest of his  remains have been thrown into the sea or  into lake rapple in what the  dictatorship called operation removal  seventeen other victims were executed  closer to home  this is the hill where the soldiers  brought them shot them dead and then  left their bodies to decompose the  squadron was led by a lieutenant colonel  magana but he will never spend a single  day in prison he died a year ago  peacefully in his bed before he could be  sentenced  many of the victims families have also  died that's why sonia valenzuela still  feels sadness  justice that takes so long she says  still feels like injustice  lucia newman al jazeera by nature

